<%# The questions podcasters (and judges) ask, answered plainly. Native <details>, so it works without JavaScript. %>
<div class="faq">
  <h2 id="faq-title" class="type-h2">Questions</h2>
  <div class="faq-list">
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-question">Where does the data come from?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>
          Qloo's taste graph tells us what people with your listeners' tastes love more than most people do (brands,
          music, TV, podcasts and places) and how they lean by age and gender. Podcast Index gives us your show's details. Your RSS feed, and the
          feeds of similar shows, give us episode notes and the sponsors those shows mention.
        </p>
        <p>
          Claude, Anthropic's AI model, plans the research and writes the reasons and pitches from those results. Every brand
          in a report comes from Qloo's data (results are cached for up to a week).
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-question">Is this my real audience?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>
          No. It's modeled from Qloo taste data, not measured. We never see your listener list or your hosting stats. A
          report describes people whose tastes match your show's: what they love, where they live and how they lean. Age and
          gender are shown as leans, never as percentages.
        </p>
        <p>That's strong evidence of fit to show a brand. It isn't a headcount.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-question">What's a proxy profile?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>
          Many small shows aren't in Qloo's graph yet. When yours isn't, we build the taste profile from comparable shows,
          guests and topics found in your feed, plus any shows you tell us your listeners love. The report labels it a proxy
          profile and lists each of those seeds with where it came from.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-question">What doesn't a report prove?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>
          That a brand will say yes, or what it will pay. Fit scores rank brands by shared taste and sponsorship history; they
          aren't a forecast. The downloads you enter are self-reported, and we don't look up contact emails. The pitch pack
          tells you which role to look for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-question">Do you email brands for me?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>
          No. You send every pitch yourself: copy it, or open it in Gmail. There's no bulk sending, and <%= APP_NAME %> takes
          no commission on any deal you land.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-question">Who are the sample reports about?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>
          Public podcasts, researched by the same agent you'd use. Each one is labelled “Sample — not affiliated with the
          show.” The shows didn't ask for them and aren't connected to <%= APP_NAME %>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-question">Can I cancel?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>
          Yes. Monthly and yearly plans cancel from your account page and run to the end of the period you paid for. The
          <%= Entitlements.limits(:sprint)[:name] %> plan is a single payment and never renews.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary class="faq-question">What happens to my data?</summary>
      <div class="faq-answer">
        <p>
          Your shows, reports, pitches and notes are visible only to you, unless you publish a media kit. Deleting your
          account deletes all of it. The <%= link_to "privacy page", privacy_path, class: "link" %> has the details.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>
